\details{
\code{how = "cf"} (the default), the control function: the first-stage
residuals are added to the Poisson pseudo-likelihood fit with the same
fixed effects and offset. The residual takes up the part of the exposure
that moves with unmeasured causes of the outcome; its own coefficient
tests whether the exposure was endogenous at all. With a log link this
is consistent when the first-stage residual enters the mean as a linear
index (Wooldridge 2014), and it is the default for that reason.

\code{how = "plug"}, the fitted exposure (the exposure less its first-stage
residual) in place of the exposure in the Poisson fit: the second stage
as Bondy, Roth and Sager (2020, equation 3, p. 565) write it, with
"AQI-hat". With a log link the plug-in is not consistent in general
(the mean of \code{exp()} of a fitted value is not \code{exp()} of the mean); it
is offered to reproduce the paper's specification.

Standard errors (\code{se}): \code{"boot"} (the default), a cluster bootstrap that
refits both stages on each of \code{nboot} draws of the clusters of \code{clus},
made on the R side with R's generator (so \code{set.seed()} or \code{seed}
reproduce them), each draw weighting a cluster by how often it was
drawn; \code{"naive"}, the clustered errors of the second-stage Poisson fit
alone, which treat the first-stage residual or fitted value as data and
are therefore wrong (too small, as a rule): they are given only to
reproduce papers that report them. Bondy, Roth and Sager do not say how
their second-stage errors account for the first stage. The exponential
generalised method of moments of Mullahy (1997) and Windmeijer and
Santos Silva (1997), which needs no first-stage model, is \code{\link[=expgmm]{expgmm()}}.
The negative binomial second stage (\code{family = "nb"}). Feigenbaum and
Muller (2016, secs. 5-6 and table 2) model city homicides as negative
binomial with the population as the exposure (\code{expo}) and instrument a
city's use of lead pipes by its rail distance to the nearest lead
refinery, estimating "the IV negative binomial regressions following the
control function procedure described by Cameron and Trivedi (2013, p.
401-406)" with errors from "a bootstrap resampling at the city level".
Here the first stage is as above, by least squares on the instruments,
the controls and the fixed effects; the second stage is the negative
binomial maximum-likelihood fit (variance \code{mu + mu^2 / theta}, \code{theta}
estimated, as \code{MASS::glm.nb()} fits it) of the outcome on the
endogenous columns, the controls, the first-stage residuals, an
intercept and the fixed effects as indicator columns (not absorbed: a
negative binomial fit has no within transformation, and many levels
bring the incidental-parameter bias of a nonlinear model); \code{se = "boot"}
refits both stages on each draw of the clusters (rows of a cluster
drawn twice enter twice), \code{"naive"} takes the clustered sandwich of the
second stage alone. Separation is checked as for the Poisson fit. A
systematic review of individual-level studies of lead and crime is
Talayero et al. (2023); see \code{\link[=cemtch]{cemtch()}}.

Karamik and von Graevenitz (2022, eqs 2-3) use the control function
with the wind sectors of \code{\link[=windiv]{windiv()}} as instruments (one set per region),
area, weekday and month-year effects, the group's population as the
offset (\code{expo}) and a bootstrap of both stages with the groups as
clusters and 1,000 draws (\code{se = "boot"}, \code{nboot = 1000}); Batkeyev and
DeRemer (2023, appendix) report the same design beside their linear one.

Separation is handled as in \code{\link[=ppmlfe]{ppmlfe()}} (groups of zeros dropped), in the
full sample and in each draw; a draw that cannot be fitted (an
instrument without variation in it, say) is left out and counted in
\code{nfail}. The first-stage table is that of \code{\link[=ivfe]{ivfe()}}, clustered by \code{clus}.

\code{plot()} draws the rate ratios with their intervals on a log
scale and the first-stage F statistics beside the 5\% critical value
of the effective F; it returns a list of what it drew (\code{coef},
\code{first}).
